gners or engineers just to name a  few  we ended up tracking 110 000  unique actions that these companies took  related to design  and the exam question uh was well is  there any correlation  between those who are consistently  performing best in terms of design  actions  and consistently performing best  financially well spoiler alert the  answer is yes  otherwise mark and i would not be having  this conversation today  but we we were surprised by the level  of correlation this graph here uh  what this shows is that for the  companies who consistently perform best  in terms of uh their design actions  they were growing their revenues at  almost twice the rate of their industry  peers  and on the shareholder return side it  was pretty stark as well  70 higher shareholder return growth  than their industry peers that's what  this is about  and that's why we take this topic now so  seriously  it's wonderful that mackenzie has  actually undertaken this research and  managed to concretely actually link  design to better business  outcomes at design singapore we also see  good design practices  such as a focus on customer experience  and quick prototyping and iteration  as a cornerstone to actually helping  organizations  drive a more agile and innovative  culture  your research has actually established  that design is not something  vague or amorphous right it's actually  something that would deliver better  business performance  if companies take actually concrete  steps towards  embedding that deeply within the  organizations so what do you think is  actually critical for companies to  become top performers  and design and reap the financial  rewards that come with it  yeah that's the exciting bit what what  do you have to do what's the magic  recipe mark to uh to start making better  products and services  firstly i should say one's definition of  design matters it's one of those words  like um  strategy or big data that seems to mean  different things to different people  the companies who were treating design  most effectively  understood that the modern definition is  understanding user needs  and then creating solutions to meet  those needs so that means it can be  digital design physical design  service design but i want to say that up  front because i think there are still  there's some people who think of design  as being color material finish and it's  it's far from from that once you've  understood it  out of 000 design actions that were  taken  there's actually remarkably few that  consistently lead to better performance  for example number of designers very  little correlation with improved  performance  creating a shiny new design center very  little correlation with improved  performance  hiring a senior designer from from  another company  very little uh correlation improved  performance there were only four themes  that consistently made a difference and  we'll talk through  each of those in turn what i would say  before we cover each of those for though  mark is  the colleagues in singapore who have  already taken part  in this exercise have shared with us  that whilst the four that we're about to  talk about are common sense they're not  common practice  we originally asked over a hundred  companies in singapore  do you think that from a design point of  view you're in a good place  uh at the beginning of the interview  eighty percent of them said yes they  said yes i think we're doing things  right from a design point of view  when we actually benchmark them against  those 300 international companies  only 10 of those singaporean companies  were in the top and second quartile only  10  and what that means is a huge  opportunity here particularly for  singapore  to jump on these four themes and make a  real difference  those are certainly some super  insightful statistics  and that's certainly one of the reasons  why we saw value actually in the design  index  and it really kind of provides a  data-driven frame of reference when we  talk about design performance that  in the past has been rather abstract we  all may think that we are doing well in  design but it's actually important to  have a common language  and a point of reference to actually  identify areas of improvement and growth  yeah i fully agree with you and let's  talk about the the four i mean  the first one uh is is really around  leadership  um and this is one but we've had great  conversations with many many companies  in singapore  the marker of companies who are doing  well from a design leadership point of  view  are those who treat design  leadership with the same respect and  rigor that you might trip  treat strategy or or operations or or  any other more  uh mature uh leadership topic  they look at the data they make  decisions and  there's a ceo of um a med tech company  who  who said i thought in a very powerful  way he said if we don't have data  on the quality of our products on  whether it's leading to better patient  outcomes  better adherence to medication better  perceived quality of life and we have  that data to show we're doing markedly  better than a competition  than shame on us how can we be making  the right strategic decisions  unless we can be clear that our medical  device designs  are making a difference and a better  difference in others and that for me was  a really simple way of thinking about  how a ceo can think about design metrics  customer metrics patriot  patient metrics in a way that actually  leads to better strategic decisions  now the thing about creating additional  metrics and having these regular  assessments on user  outcomes sounds like it could be really  difficult for some organizations  and certainly appears so based on the  statistics from the results  what would you say to companies that  don't have the resources for this now  firstly don't panic you're in good  company and in fact in singapore of  the uh the leaders we spoke to 75  percent three out of four said they  currently don't feel they could make  objective business decisions on design  so  this is an area which is being developed  as we speak  i actually think the whole the whole  design industry is going through a bit  of a  renaissance at the moment this is like  what marketing went through in the 1990s  where it went from being this  black box this magic to people saying no  we can do  closed loop marketing we can look at roi  we can understand the metrics  and i think we're seeing that on the  metric side and design now  i'll give you one example of a company  who who i think  took this on really well and it was a  ceo of a consumer goods company who was  frustrated  that he felt that he and his top team  had lost connection with the customer  they felt that they didn't have the  right metrics  and they were just focused on pie charts  and ebitda but they'd lost that picture  on whether they actually had sustainably  better products and services  so what this individual did he walked  into his uh  his executive boardroom and he said to  his team  congratulations everybody i'm changing  your bonus  this year half your bonus will still be  tied to quarterly ebitda earnings of  course we have to deliver to the street  but the other half will be different on  christmas day  i will excuse myself from cutting the  turkey with my family  i will go and  see how our products are doing on amazon  not in terms of sales but in terms of  amazon stars out of five i'm gonna see  on average how many stars out of five  our products have  i'm gonna compare that to our leading  competition and if we have fewer stars  you don't get that second half of your  bonus am i clear  now you could say that's a very crass  measure you could say that's open to  to all sorts of gaming i'm sure everyone  opened an amazon account that night and  started  scoring things five but did it have the  intended consequence absolutely yes it  changed the culture of that company  people started talking about customers  it became a discussion in the boardroom  and i'd encourage companies in singapore  to think what's that equivalent  what's that one first customer-led  metric that they can bring to the  boardroom  that leads to a different kind of  conversation no absolutely i mean that's  a really interesting way of  getting people to focus holistically on  the customer  and really tying that to something  immediate and measurable right and just  really forcing a kind of cultural change  now that we've spoken actually about  these kind of concrete metrics what  about software aspects about  collaboration actually how teams work  together  that that was the second topic i mean  teamwork absolutely critical  we had an expectation though that the  companies that perform best were going  to be the ones who  created these wonderful design centers  these innovation centers  what we found from the research was  slightly counter-intuitively  companies who at some point seemed to  have made these huge ivory towers  committed to design actually saw a dip  in performance in the years that  followed  and what turned out to be important was  to some degree  de-emphasizing the importance of the  design department  but instead elevating the importance of  individual designers  and spending far more effort and energy  in thinking how do i effectively  integrate those designers into  cross-functional teams with engineering  with marketing  with product with finance with the  project management team as well  and it was those organizations those  institutions who were most successful  we saw this beautifully captured in a in  one company in fact it was a furniture  making company  who opened uh or planned  i should say to open a shiny design and  innovation center of two of its leading  markets  now in the second market they changed  their plan and instead they invested  that money in design  talent and better financial incentives  for those cross-functional teams  so that ever